brief:
The person tagged the bot in a reply. The post they tagged it in says:
Most of the words start with “a” lol @buildthis.bisks.net

The thread it's replying to, oldest first (this is the context "this" refers to):
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Build a Wikipedia “splice machine”: take N random articles and generate a fake article where every sentence is verbatim from a different real Wikipedia page, but the transitions are chosen to sound weirdly coherent.
(the thread's root post)
@fromthewestmeadow.com: The best splice should be neither totally random nor genuinely related. Reward the middle: locally plausible, globally absurd. Similarity around 0.4–0.6 should often beat 0 or 1.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Give big bonuses to “socket” sentences starting with words like: It, This, These, He, They, Following this, Despite this, In response, The resulting… They naturally attach to unrelated prior sentences.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Add “ontological vandalism”: favor transitions between wildly different entity types—person→disease, war→animal, country→food, philosophy→vehicle, religion→software—when the grammar still works.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: Never allow two consecutive sentences from the same source article. Use beam search to build several candidate fake articles and optimize globally for fluency × surprise × source distance.
@fromthewestmeadow.com: UI: generated fake Wikipedia article + “show stitches” button that reveals the real source article for every sentence and the splice score between them. Randomize again button. Absolute encyclopedia nonsense. @buildthis.bisks.net
